"Represents a probability distribution with equally weighted dots placed at selected quantiles and stacked into readable columns.\n\nUse when: Readers benefit from interpreting uncertainty as countable equally likely outcomes. A full distribution should be shown without a smooth density-height interpretation.\n\nAvoid when: High numeric precision or a very coarse dot count would materially misrepresent tail probability.\n\nVisual grammar: Dot position encodes a distribution quantile or its documented bin approximation. Every dot represents the same fraction of probability, with stacking used only to avoid collisions.\n\nBuild: Choose a dot count and assign equal probability mass per dot. Compute representative quantiles from the stated uncertainty distribution. Arrange equal-sized dots on the value scale and include a key such as one dot = 5%.\n\nCheck: Check total dot probability equals 100%. Verify the dot positions reflect the chosen quantile convention. State any tail truncation and binning or stacking approximation.\n\nPitfalls: The dots represent probability mass, not necessarily observed individuals. A coarse number of dots can hide rare tails; add numeric tail probabilities when they matter.\n\nExample: Show a synthetic delivery-date forecast with twenty equally weighted dots, each representing a 5% probability slice.\n\nAlternatives: Gradient interval plot, Density plot, Fan chart."
